  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/... \nBy Friday, the main upper level trough is forecast to shift\nnortheastward out of a our region leaving dry southwesterly flow\nin place. Guidance shows low level mixing ratios quickly dropping\nto 5-7 g/kg across the lower deserts to only 6-8 g/kg over the\nArizona high terrain. As a result, Friday will begin another dry\nperiod with only lingering chances for some isolated showers and\nmaybe a few thunderstorms over the eastern Arizona high terrain.\nTemperatures are forecast to remain quite stable over the next few\ndays with highs right around to slightly above normal and lows a \nfew degrees above normal.\n\nWesterly dry flow should persist through at least Saturday with\nlarge scale troughing remaining to our northwest while the \nsubtropical ridge slowly shifts southwestward from east Texas \ninto the interior of Mexico. This shift in the ridge is likely to\nbring higher heights temporarily for our region later Friday \nthrough Saturday, so daytime highs Saturday are likely to top out\nbetween 100-104 degrees (4-6 degrees above normal) under mostly \nsunny skies.\n\nForecast uncertainty increases dramatically going into early next\nweek as it will likely depend upon what happens with Hurricane\nPolo, the positioning of the subtropical ridge, and an upper \nlevel trough that may swing through our region on or around next \nMonday. The latest NHC track for Polo has it slowly making its way\nwestward over the next few days before turning more to the north \nthis weekend, but staying west of southern Baja. This track may be\nenough to help advect tropical moisture northward as far as our \narea, but uncertainty remains quite high. There is a low end \npotential of tropical moisture making its way northward into our \narea just before the aforementioned trough swings through our \nregion and this could lead to chances for strong to severe storms\nalong with heavy rain potential on or around Monday. The more \nlikely scenario would involve modest moisture making its way into \nour area, but still with the support of the trough. The trough may\nstill be strong enough to support some strong to severe storms \ndue to much higher than normal shear, but with less moisture it \nwould lower the severe threat as well as well as the threat for \nheavy rainfall. At this moment, we are unsure which solution will \nwin out, but we expect to have a better idea by the weekend.",
